// CSE-CIC-IDS2018 processed CICFlowMeter CSVs: REAL dirty security logs on anonymous S3
// (ca-central-1). The workload binds the 8 standard-header (80-column, LF) files via an explicit
// comma-list of URIs (the engine's multi-location resource form); the 84-column CRLF file and the mixed-schema glob are dirty-data cases, not
// workload. Real-world dirt asserted BY these expectations, not worked around: embedded mid-file
// header rows read as data rows (null numeric fields, literal 'Label'/'Timestamp' strings),
// dd/MM/yyyy timestamp STRINGS (lexical min/max), Excel-truncated files (2^20-1 rows), and
// Infinity/NaN rate columns (excluded from arithmetic). Expected tables derived at authoring time
// by clickhouse local reading every column as String with *OrNull casts - the exact mirror of
// retain-row/null-field semantics (see each query's notes). Every test allows (not requires)
// warnings: dirty reads legitimately warn per unparseable value, warning text varies with node
// topology and caps, and the VALUES are the assertion here. Tightening to warning families is a
// recorded follow-up once a green run catalogues the exact texts.

// corpus: cse-cic-ids2018
